Q: Core Dumped after copying string to 2D Array A: <p>What you are using is not a real 2D array (i.e. an array of arrays), but rather an array of pointers to arrays. If you want a real 2D array, then you only need a single call of <code>malloc</code>. You only need the following <code>malloc</code> call:</p>
<pre><code>char (*list)[COLS] = malloc(ROWS * sizeof list[0]);
</code></pre>
<p>This will make <code>list</code> a pointer to an array of <code>COLS</code> elements of type <code>char</code>, and it will point to the first row of the 2D array (i.e. to the first element of the outer array). This is what we want, because in C, we need a pointer to the first element of the array in order to use that pointer for indexing into the array.</p>
<p>However, if you use a real 2D array, you cannot mark the end of the array with a <code>NULL</code> pointer, because you are not working with pointers. You will need some other way of keeping track of the number of valid strings in the 2D array. For example, you can use an <code>int</code> variable.</p>
<p>Here is an example:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

#define ROWS 12
#define COLS 24

int num_strings = 0;

void addToList( char (*list)[COLS], char *string )
{
    if ( num_strings == ROWS )
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Error: Cannot add string because array is full!\n&quot; );
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    if ( snprintf( list[num_strings], sizeof list[num_strings], &quot;%s&quot;, string ) &gt;= COLS )
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Error: Cannot add string because it is too large!\n&quot; );
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    num_strings++;
}

void printList( char (*list)[COLS] )
{
    for ( int i = 0 ; i &lt; num_strings; i++ )
    {
        fprintf( stdout, &quot;%s\n&quot;, list[i] );
    }
}

int main()
{
    char (*list)[COLS] = malloc(ROWS * sizeof list[0]);
    if( list == NULL)
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Error: Memory allocation failure!\n&quot;);
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    addToList( list, &quot;First Line&quot; );
    addToList( list, &quot;Second Line&quot; );

    printList( list );

    free( list );

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This program has the following output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>First Line
Second Line
</code></pre>
<p>However, having an array of pointers to arrays can be useful too, as it allows you to use a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jagged_array" rel="nofollow noreferrer">jagged array</a>, so that strings that are significantly shorter than 24 characters don't always take 24 characters of memory. Here is an example:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

#define MAX_STRINGS 12

void addToList( char **list, char *string )
{
    int i;

    // find end of list
    for ( i = 0; list[i] != NULL; i++ )
    {
    }

    if ( i &gt;= MAX_STRINGS )
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Error: Array already full!\n&quot; );
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    // NOTE: This function requires C23 or later
    list[i] = strdup( string );
    if ( list[i] == NULL )
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Error: Memory allocation failure!\n&quot;);
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    // mark the new end of the list
    list[i+1] = NULL;
}

void printList( char **list )
{
    for ( int i = 0 ; list[i] != NULL; i++ )
    {
        fprintf( stdout, &quot;%s\n&quot;, list[i] );
    }
}

void freeList( char **list )
{
    for ( int i = 0 ; list[i] != NULL; i++ )
    {
        free( list[i] );
    }
}

int main()
{
    char **list = malloc( ( MAX_STRINGS + 1) * sizeof list[0] );
    if( list == NULL)
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Error: Memory allocation failure!\n&quot;);
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    // mark the list as empty
    list[0] = NULL;

    addToList( list, &quot;First Line&quot; );
    addToList( list, &quot;Second Line&quot; );

    printList( list );

    freeList( list );

    free( list );

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This program has the same output as the first program.</p>
<p>In this example, it is unlikely that you will be actually saving memory by using a jagged array, because <code>malloc</code> needs some memory for bookkeeping. However, if the maximum length of the strings is signficantly larger, then it is likely that you will save memory.</p>

